WEBVTT

00:00:00.100 --> 00:00:03.166
Threat hunters rely on many sources of information.

00:00:03.166 --> 00:00:07.382
These may include EDR telemetry, SIEM logs, authentication events, DNS activity,

00:00:07.382 --> 00:00:11.215
firewall records, cloud logs, proxy logs, endpoint artifacts, network telemetry,

00:00:11.215 --> 00:00:12.365
and threat intelligence.

00:00:12.365 --> 00:00:18.114
The quality of a hunt often depends on the quality and completeness of available data.

00:00:18.114 --> 00:00:20.031
Missing visibility creates blind spots.

00:00:20.031 --> 00:00:24.630
A hypothesis cannot be tested reliably if the organization does not collect

00:00:24.630 --> 00:00:27.313
the telemetry needed to observe the behavior.

00:00:27.313 --> 00:00:28.463
Context also matters.

00:00:28.463 --> 00:00:32.679
An unusual process on one system may be normal on another.

00:00:32.679 --> 00:00:38.428
A login pattern may be expected for one role but suspicious for a different user,

00:00:38.428 --> 00:00:39.961
location, time, or system.

00:00:39.961 --> 00:00:44.178
Hunters combine technical evidence with environmental knowledge to make informed judgments.

00:00:44.178 --> 00:00:46.477
They look for patterns, timelines, relationships,

00:00:46.477 --> 00:00:51.460
and exceptions that help distinguish normal operations from activity that needs further investigation.
